<section>
    <h3>What is redacted is replayed redacted</h3>
    <pre style="font-size: 0.45em;"><code class="language-json" data-trim>
{"access_token":"[REDACTED]","id_token":"[REDACTED]","token_type":"Bearer"}
    </code></pre>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.65em;">
        <li class="fragment">A test that verifies the signature of a replayed <code>id_token</code> will fail</li>
        <li class="fragment">Solution: <code>redact_except</code>, or your own <code>RedactorInterface</code></li>
        <li class="fragment">And in that case: review the file before committing it</li>
    </ul>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Second pitfall, which follows directly from redaction: what is redacted is replayed redacted. If your file contains "access_token":"[REDACTED]", that's exactly what your code will receive on replay.</p>
        <p>[click] So a test that verifies the signature of a replayed id_token will fail. [REDACTED] is not a valid JWT.</p>
        <p>[click] The solution: redact_except for that field, or your own RedactorInterface.</p>
        <p>[click] But in that case, you're turning off a protection. So review the file before committing it, and make sure what you let through really is a test token.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
